abstract: >-
  A technical note that transposes the two-dimensional finite-volume doubling criterion of OpenAI to SU(2) lattice gauge theories in dimensions four and three.
  Under symmetry and a positive trace-class transfer representation, Delta(n) = 16 log Z(n^4) - log Z((2n)^4) <= 2^-9 implies Delta(2n) <= 256 Delta(n)^2
  and a bound on every excited transfer eigenvalue; if the bound holds uniformly at a fixed physical scale and the lattice Osterwalder-Schrader forms converge
  (assumed), the limit has a mass gap of at least (log 2)/ell under the Osterwalder-Schrader reconstruction (assumed). The implication is formalised in Lean 4
  against the author's own statement of the problem, with named hypotheses. No estimate at weak coupling, no existence of the continuum limit, and no application
  to the formalised construction of OpenAI is claimed. Version 5.0.0 of the series of Bhattacharjee et al. (doi:10.5281/zenodo.24147967), which is earlier,
  contains the same doubling defect for four-dimensional tori and a one-scale criterion whose hypothesis implies ours and whose conclusion is wider;
  no priority is claimed. The principle that an inequality between partition functions of finite periodic tori implies decay is classical (Tomboulis-Yaffe 1985; Kovacs-Tomboulis 2002; Kanazawa 2008). Version 1.2 (12 November 2026) adds the reverse implication: the doubling test Delta(n) <= 2^-9 implies the one-scale bound (B*) of that series at the scale 16n and a gap on every integer cube, so the two criteria are equivalent up to the scale; a bridge corollary to their Theorem F is conditional and not verified by us; this is not a proof of the mass gap, and no priority is claimed for the one-scale criterion. Version 1.3 (12 November 2026) adds, on paper and with abstract Lean checks of the finite algebra only: the decomposition of the periodic trace over electric-flux sectors, the proof that the criterion implies disorder of the 't Hooft twists on every hypercube (a length, not an area), the equivalence of the criterion with two one-scale inequalities, a conditional necessary scale, and a proof that the criterion fails at every fixed lattice size as the coupling goes to infinity (constants not explicit; anteriority not settled). Predominantly a transposition; not refereed; no human has checked the proofs.
